# This package's own releases are exempt from the global min-release-age guard.
#
# `min-release-age=7` in ~/.npmrc refuses anything published in the last week —
# a supply-chain guard worth keeping, and it should keep applying to everything
# ELSE. But it also hides OUR OWN package from us the moment we publish it:
# `npm create @retreever/rapper` failed with ENOVERSIONS for a package that had
# just gone live. The workaround was pasting `--min-release-age=0` onto every
# command, which is a flag a user should never have to know about.
#
# min-release-age-exclude is npm's own allowlist for exactly this. Note that
# `npm create` does NOT honour it (npm 11.18 and 12.0.2, checked 3 Sep 2026) —
# only install/update/outdated/query do — so a fresh `npm create` still needs
# `--min-release-age=0` BEFORE the package name until that lands upstream.
min-release-age-exclude=@retreever/create-rapper
